  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1258 AM EDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nThis morning will be the coolest of the upcoming week with \nwidespread lows in the low 50s. It is possible that some of our \nrural, low lying areas see a low in the upper 40s this morning. NBM \nprobabilities currently have about 20-35% chances this morning, \nwhile chances are less than 5% for Fort Wayne and South Bend, where \nlows will be a touch higher, likely closer to 51-52 degrees.\n\nTranquil weather continues today with dry conditions and low \nhumidity. High pressure current centered over the Upper Midwest will \nslowly build across the Great Lakes in the coming days. Dry \nconditions are expected to continue through Saturday with this high \npressure system eventually overhead. High temperatures today will be \na bit warmer than yesterday but still only topping out in the upper \n70s! Today and tonight will be great opportunities to open up \nwindows, with temperatures dropping into the 50s once again \novernight. Lows in the 50s, while unusual from the heat and humidity \nwe have been experiencing recently, are not that uncommon in July. \nWe typically average about 5 nights with lows below 60 degrees in \nJuly. \n\nTemperatures trend much warmer into the weekend. Moisture increases \nas WAA ramps up, which means humidity starts increasing too. Hot and \nhumid conditions return by Sunday and Monday with highs in the \nupper 80s to low 90s with dewpoints in the low to mid 70s. \nIncreased evapotranspiration from crops may also aid in locally \nhigher dewpoints. Our forecast area will be split in between a \ntrough to our east and a large upper level ridge to our west. \nPattern recognition tells me this is a favorable set up for a \npotentially active pattern into early next week with northwest \nflow aloft. A frontal system brings the next chances for \nrain/storms late Sunday into Monday, although confidence is \nuncertain on exact timing. Models have been trending drier for \nSunday with precipitation chances that currently sit around 20%;\nMonday's chances are higher at 20-60%. Any storms that develop \nmay be able to ride the edge of the upper level ridge through \nour area. If we can get sufficent instability and moisture, \nsevere weather is not out of the question for Sunday and \nespecially Monday. Confidence is very low given the high spread \nin deterministic and ensemble model guidance.",
